- The data model has four exposed types: Scene, Curve, Fill and Vertex
- A Curve is a cubic bezier curve with two end points (Vertexes) and two control points
- A Fill is defined by a list of of one or more closed loops of curves. If there are more than one, each closed loop after the first is a hole.
- Multiple Curves may share a Vertex
- The operations are:
    1. Add a curve to the scene
    2. Attempt to add a fill at a point (may fail if point is not fully enclosed) (updates the color of an existing fill if there is already a fill at that point)
    3. Delete a curve (deletes any fills which reference it)
    4. Delete a fill
    5. Replace a curve with a new curve
    6. Move a vertex to different coordinates
- For operations 1, 5 and 6, the following rules apply in order:
    1. CURVES MAY NOT SELF-INTERSECT. If a curve crosses itself, it is split at the intersection to form three curves (the segment before the intersection, the segment after the intersection, and the loop)
    2. CURVES MAY NOT INTERSECT OTHER CURVES. If a curve crosses another curve, both curves are SPLIT at the intersection and a new Vertex is created there which is shared by the sub-curves.
    3. SNAPPING. If two Vertexes are less than SNAP_DISTANCE apart then they are merged into a single Vertex which is shared by all affected curves.
    4. FILLS. For any fills which referenced curves affected by the prior three rules, each of the replacements should be reflected in its list. If any loop now revisits a vertex, that section should get split off into a new loop (ie a hole). If any vertexes in the scene are now inside the fill and are not in its list of loops of curves, for each of those vertexes, determine all non-overlapping loops which it is part of and add them to the fill as holes.

- This should be a simple library, under 1500 LOC. Don't overcomplicate it.
- TESTING:
Draw N curves, randomly. Perform operations randomly, but make all fills transparent. Then, using a canvas library, draw the curves (with no antialiasing) to the raster canvas. Attempt to add a visible fill at some point via the library, and in parallel perform a raster flood fill from the same point on the raster canvas. Re-render the scene and verify that it is pixel-identical to the raster canvas. If it isn't, simplify the scene until there is a minimal test case and then solve the problem. If it is, repeat. Success criteria is 10k successful tests in a row.
